@@s010 Missing data, outliers and the multivariate normal
@@s011 Lecture 8 (data cleaning) and the exercise of week 9 <i>Descriptive statistics and handling missing data</i>, with the poses of 200 sequences. What to do with the values that are missing, how an outlier is recognised and why the multivariate normal distribution is the reference model. Exam questions <b>Q7 (c) and (d)</b>. Readings: <i>MissingData.pdf</i> and <i>Multivariate_Normal_Distribution.pdf</i> (folder Reading/Week8).
@@s012 Why data are missing
@@s013 Type
@@s014 It means
@@s015 Example
@@s016 <b>MCAR</b>: missing completely at random
@@s017 the absence depends on nothing
@@s018 a file corrupted at random; in the exercise 30% of the values removed at random
@@s019 <b>MAR</b>: missing at random
@@s020 depends on <b>other</b> observed variables
@@s021 the joints of the hand are missing more often when the arm is behind the back
@@s022 <b>NMAR</b>: not missing at random
@@s023 depends on the missing value itself
@@s024 whoever earns a lot does not declare the salary
@@s025 With NMAR no imputation is truly correct: the missing values are different from the ones you see.
@@s026 MCAR, MAR, NMAR
@@s027 How to fill them in
@@s028 Strategy
@@s029 Pros
@@s030 Cons
@@s031 remove the rows
@@s032 simple, no invented data
@@s033 data are lost; if it is not MCAR the dataset becomes biased
@@s034 zero or a constant
@@s035 immediate
@@s036 invents values that may be very far from the truth
@@s037 mean / median of the column
@@s038 fast
@@s039 ignores the correlations, <b>reduces the variance</b>
@@s040 most frequent value
@@s041 works also for categories
@@s042 can introduce bias
@@s043 k-NN, MICE, regression
@@s044 use the other variables
@@s045 slower; k-NN sensitive to outliers
@@s046 <b>interpolation</b> over time
@@s047 uses the nearby values in the sequence
@@s048 only for time series
@@s049 The exercise with the real data: 200 sequences × 30 frames × 50 coordinates, 30% NaN
@@s050 The coordinates are worth on average about 0.1 (between 0 and 0.43 for the joint looked at): putting 0 moves the joint far from the body.
@@s051 mean of the feature: MSE = <mark class="ora">0.00393</mark>
@@s052 Every missing coordinate becomes the mean position of that joint over all the poses: reasonable, but it is an "average pose", not the true one.
@@s053 interpolation over time: MSE = <mark class="ok">0.00134</mark>
@@s054 Between two nearby frames the body moves little: the line between the value before and the one after estimates the missing value well. It uses the <b>structure</b> of the data.
@@s055 after imputing with the mean, the variance of a feature drops to <mark class="no">71%</mark>
@@s056 The 30% of values was placed exactly at the centre: the data seem less variable than they are. The statistics computed afterwards (variance, covariance) come out falsified.
@@s057 The first two MSEs coincide with those printed in the notebook. The interpolation is computed with <code>np.interp</code> (line between the known values, at the edges the nearest known value): your implementation may give a slightly different number. Note: the text speaks of 252 features, but the shape of the files is (200, 30, 25, 2), that is 25 joints × 2 = 50 coordinates.
@@s058 Zeros, mean, interpolation
@@s059 Recognising outliers
@@s060 Method
@@s061 Rule
@@s062 Limit
@@s063 <b>z-score</b>
@@s064 z = (x − μ) / σ; outlier if |z| exceeds a threshold. The tutorial uses 2 and finds 29 outliers for the age and 38 for the ticket; with the most common threshold, 3, they are 2 and 20 (verified)
@@s065 μ and σ are moved by the outliers themselves
@@s066 <b>IQR</b> (box plot)
@@s067 outlier if below Q1 − 1.5·IQR or above Q3 + 1.5·IQR
@@s068 looks at one variable at a time
@@s069 <b>Mahalanobis</b>
@@s070 needs a good estimate of the covariance C
@@s071 Same Euclidean distance, different Mahalanobis
@@s072 Distances computed with NumPy (covariance with n − 1 of the 10 points).
@@s073 Outlier does not mean error
@@s074 Before removing it, ask where it comes from: a measurement error (the reflection taken for the pupil) can be corrected or removed; a rare but true case (a fraud, an illness) is often exactly what one is interested in. See also <a href="w06-rumore-generalizzazione.html">noise and generalisation</a>.
@@s075 The multivariate normal
@@s076 From one variable to many
@@s077 The bell. That is why |z| &gt; 3 is an outlier threshold: it happens 3 times in 1000.
@@s078 nD: 𝒩(<mark class="dato">μ</mark>, <mark class="due">Σ</mark>) μ mean vector, Σ covariance matrix
@@s079 Each variable on its own is normal, but the variables can be correlated: Σ contains variances (diagonal) and covariances (off the diagonal).
@@s080 the level curves are <mark class="ok">ellipses</mark>, tilted by the correlation
@@s081 Without correlation: an ellipse with axes parallel to the axes (or a circle). With strong correlation: a narrow, diagonal ellipse, like the height-weight cloud. Mahalanobis measures the distance in "number of ellipses".
@@s082 to generate correlated data: x = <mark class="ora">L</mark> z, with L Lᵀ = Σ (Cholesky)
@@s083 The method of the reading: from two independent standard normals one obtains data with the wanted covariance. Tried with target correlation 0.75: obtained 0.752 on 5000 points.
@@s084 Why it matters for machine learning
@@s085 The noise is often modelled as normal (Y = f + N, N ~ 𝒩(0, σ²)). The covariance of the multivariate normal is the same matrix that PCA decomposes (week 12): its eigenvectors are the axes of the ellipse. And the points outside the ellipse are the outliers.
@@s086 Q7 (c) and (d)
@@s087 (c) "Describe noise, outliers, and missing data." (d) "Uncertainty, noise, data cleaning … (Covariance, distributions (e.g Normal/Gaussian))". To bring: the three types of missing data, the strategies with the results of the exercise (zeros → mean → interpolation), the side effects of imputation, z-score/IQR/Mahalanobis with the example A and B, the multivariate normal as the model of the cloud of data.
@@s088 Ellipses, eigenvectors, Cholesky
